#uuid "adc9514e-9226-52da-8468-50c2910da52d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122705} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d4b610-3729-5879-8039-1e4b7a6e253d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122706} {:answer/id #uuid "03cef378-57c1-51e4-9efd-5f572b23f587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122707} {:answer/id #uuid "066d22c3-0f85-5902-85b2-f0866f9e3ecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122708} {:answer/id #uuid "2c5c01bd-b72e-5ea0-bc98-3cc9d668d19b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03cef378-57c1-51e4-9efd-5f572b23f587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122707} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f9dd6d-472d-5254-8dcd-ad5061e10317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122704}] :question/id #uuid "f2c822bf-c206-5550-8452-95d9e9a3c934" :question/math-academy-id "q-37086" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D = P^{-1}AP$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20196e23-63f2-5208-b1ea-0d80f2c19665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306275} {:answer/id #uuid "50da3090-6e20-50de-939c-75fb166647f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306276} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4ab1ac-b69c-5165-b41d-693f838ef8e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306277} {:answer/id #uuid "e18643b7-9ad1-53e1-a2ae-17283258eb07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306278} {:answer/id #uuid "81869670-0c66-58ba-a79f-d87d79303f4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81869670-0c66-58ba-a79f-d87d79303f4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306279} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeee0811-d75d-5d71-8afe-7ee4a5918974" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc9b7c52-0fe8-51bc-a93c-2e178718ab57" :question/math-academy-id "q-37609" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-3I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable."}]}
